course: adult-life
lesson: 53
title: Breakfast and chat shows: celebrity gossip on the sofa
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Breakfast shows and chat shows feel like friends chatting on a comfy sofa, but they are a job: the presenters are paid, the chat is planned, a voice in their earpiece tells them what comes next, and the guests usually come to sell a film, a book or a song.
explain: Mixed in with the news and the weather is celebrity gossip: who is dating whom, who is splitting up, who has a new haircut. A lot of it is guessing, and the famous person did not choose to have it on TV.
explain: Vivid example: in a kitchen in Cork, Mum watches the breakfast show while Oscar eats his cereal. The presenter giggles about a singer's "secret heartbreak". Mum laughs too, then says, "Ah, I shouldn't laugh, really. Sure, she's a person like anyone."
explain: A bit of light gossip is fun, and most grown-ups enjoy it. The line is crossed when it becomes unkind, untrue, about someone's body, or about things that are private.
fact: Breakfast-show presenters are paid professionals working from a running order and an earpiece; the friendly chat is planned.
fact: Stars go on chat shows mainly to promote a new film, book or tour, and their funny stories are often practised beforehand.
fact: Gossip about a famous person's love life is entertainment, not real news, and much of it is guessing from photos.
fact: Asking someone about their weight or guessing that someone is pregnant from a photo is unkind; bodies are their own business, famous or not.
fact: Paparazzi photos are taken without permission; many famous people hate being followed, and their homes are private, just like ours.
fact: "Sources close to the couple" usually means nobody will put their name to the story; it is a rumour, not a fact.
fact: Celebrity "feuds" may be real, exaggerated or even staged for publicity; fans who take sides do not know the stars at all.
fact: A famous person crying on TV is a real person in pain; asking how you would feel if your sad moment were shown to millions is a good test.
fact: Knowing all the names at a royal wedding comes from watching TV and reading magazines; it is not the same as knowing the people.
fact: Arguments on TV get people watching, so some are allowed or even planned; that does not make shouting a good idea.
fact: Photos online are often smoothed with filters; real skin has lines and spots, and nobody looks like a filter.
fact: A public apology is judged by what the person does next; saying sorry in front of millions is hard.
fact: Studio audiences are warmed up by a comedian and sometimes extra laughter is added later, so laughter does not prove a joke is funny.
fact: Footballers and other stars are trained to say safe, boring things, because one wrong word can become a headline.
exercise: Alfie thinks the breakfast-show presenters are best friends who just chat every morning. What is true? | They are paid professionals following a plan and an earpiece | They are best friends who just happen to be filmed by chance every morning | They are viewers picked at random each morning | They make the whole show up as they go along
exercise: The breakfast show does five minutes on a singer's new boyfriend. Is that important news? | No; it's light entertainment, often guesswork | Yes; it is the most important news of the whole day | Yes; the singer asked them to talk about it on air | No; the boyfriend is always an actor
exercise: An actor tells a hilarious story about forgetting his lines. Why is he on the chat show? | To promote his new film | Because he happened to be passing by the studio | Because the host is his brother | Because actors must appear once a year
exercise: A host asks a famous woman about her weight. What do many people think about that question? | It is unkind; bodies are their own business | It is fine to ask because she chose to be famous | It is a normal question for any guest to answer | It is kind, because it shows interest
exercise: Blurry photos show a footballer on holiday. Did he want them taken? | Probably not; they were taken without permission | Yes; footballers ask photographers to follow them about | Yes; he took them himself for the show | It does not matter what he wanted
exercise: "Sources close to the couple say they are splitting up." What does Poppy actually know? | Only that an unnamed somebody said it | That they are definitely getting divorced this year | That the couple told the TV themselves | That a court has ended the marriage
exercise: Two stars insult each other online and Max takes sides against his friend. What does Dad point out? | Neither boy knows the stars, and the feud may be staged | Max must win the argument for his star, whatever happens | The feud proves that one of the stars is a really bad person | Friends should always share their star's enemies
exercise: A famous person cries on a chat show about their divorce. Sophie wants to watch the clip again. What does Mum ask? | How would you feel if your sad moment was shown to everyone? | Why not watch it five more times instead and learn the words? | Did you see how many people watched it? | Shall we send the clip to all your friends?
exercise: Grandma knows every name at the royal wedding. Does she know them personally? | No; she just watches a lot of TV and reads magazines | Yes; she went to school with the bride, years and years ago | Yes; everybody who watches gets to meet them | No; she makes up the names as she goes
exercise: A presenter gets angry at a guest. Lily is sure he'll be sacked. What is true? | Arguments get people watching, so some are even allowed | Presenters who shout are always sacked the next day | The guest will be sent to prison for it | Shouting at guests is the whole point of the show
exercise: Mum laughs at a gossip show then says "I shouldn't laugh, really". Is gossip good or bad? | A bit of light gossip is fine; spreading hurtful things is not | All gossip is evil and Mum should be punished for laughing at it | All gossip is fine as long as it's on TV | Gossip is only bad if you get caught
exercise: A singer looks very different on TV from her photos online. Why? | The online photos were smoothed with filters | She has gone ugly since last week, for some reason | The TV makes everyone look worse on purpose | It must be a different singer on TV
exercise: A famous person says sorry on TV for a mistake. How do we know if he meant it? | By what he does afterwards | By how many tears he cried | By how famous he still is | We can never know anything about it
exercise: A "shocking" new haircut gets a whole segment. Why is it on TV? | The programme had time to fill | A haircut is very big news | The hairdresser paid for the news | It was dangerous to the public
exercise: The chat show audience laughs at every single joke, even bad ones. Why? | A warm-up comedian gets them laughing, and laughs can be added | Every joke on TV is truly hilarious, or it would never be allowed on | The audience is paid to cry otherwise | The jokes get funnier in the studio
exercise: A footballer gives a dull interview full of polite nothing. Why? | Stars are careful because one wrong word becomes a headline | He is a boring person with nothing at all to say about anything | Footballers are not allowed to speak English | The interviewer told him to be dull
exercise: The TV shows a famous person's house. Isla wants to go and look. What does Mum say? | It would feel scary for them, like strangers outside our house | It's fine, famous people love visitors at the door, it's part of fame | Go, but only take a few photos | Go, because it was on TV so it's public
exercise: A presenter guesses an actress is pregnant from a photo, and Charlie tells his class. What is wrong? | It's a guess about her body, which is unkind and may be untrue | Nothing; presenters always know the truth about the people they talk about | Nothing, as long as he says it quietly | Only that he told the wrong class
test: Mia's mate: "Saw it on telly, that boy band are ALL secretly brothers, swear." Her cousin checks: they aren't. What happened? | A story grew and changed as it was passed on | The band are lying about their families to the fans | The TV was hiding the truth on purpose | Her cousin checked the wrong band online
test: Freya laughs at an old clip of a presenter falling off a stage years ago. Next day a girl at school is teased about an old mistake. What links them? | Laughing at old mistakes can hurt, on TV or in the playground | Nothing; famous people's mistakes are different and don't count | It's only teasing if it's on TV | The girl should have been on TV too
test: Auntie Sue, sipping tea: "Course they're splitting, sources close to them said so on the telly this morning." How sure is it? | Not sure; nobody named has said it | Totally sure, because it was on the telly | Sure, because Auntie Sue believes it | Sure, the couple must have told them
test: Big sister: "The breakfast lot are SO fake, they don't even like each other." Mum: "Well, it's a job, love." What does Mum mean? | They are paid to be friendly on air, whatever they feel | They hate each other and fight off camera every single morning | They're all actors who never read the news | They work for free because they like the sofa
test: Ava's brother: "New haircut's on the news, must be massive." What's the more likely truth? | The programme needed to fill a few minutes | Haircuts are top news in every country | The haircut broke the law somehow | The singer begged the news to cover it
test: Grandad, grumbling: "That host asked her how much she weighs, live on telly! Disgraceful." Who is right? | Grandad; people's bodies are their own business | The host; famous people must answer anything they're asked | Nobody; weight is a normal thing to ask | The host; it was only a joke, so it's fine
test: Leo: "Everyone was cracking up, so the jokes must've been well funny." What could be going on? | A warm-up act and added laughter make the audience laugh | Studio audiences are always the funniest people in the country | Jokes on TV are tested on scientists first | The audience is laughing at Leo at home
test: Mum, watching a star apologise: "Hm. Let's see what he does next week." Why does she say that? | An apology is judged by what someone does next | She wants to watch him on TV again next week | She thinks next week's show will be funnier | She is sure he will say sorry twice
test: Dad: "Two pop stars at war online again, I bet their managers love it." What does Dad suspect? | The feud may be helping them get attention | The managers are fighting each other too | Pop stars are not allowed online at all | The war is between two real countries
test: Max, watching Grandma name every royal guest: "Do you know the King, Nan?!" Nan laughs. Why? | She knows names from TV and magazines, not the people | She does know him, but it's a secret | She used to work in the palace kitchen | She is only pretending to know the names
test: Theo's mate: "Footballers are thick, they never say anything interesting." What is the fairer view? | They are trained to be careful, because words get twisted | He's right; footballers cannot think at all, they only kick | Footballers are banned from talking about football to anybody | They save interesting things for their pets
test: Evie, staring at a magazine: "Why does she look all spotty on telly? She's gone rank." Put her right. | Real skin has spots; her online photos were filtered | She has caught a terrible illness overnight and nobody knows | The TV cameras paint spots on people | Spotty skin means she stopped being famous
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which celebrated persons customarily appear as guests upon television chat programmes. | The promotion of a forthcoming film, book or tour | The fulfilment of a legal obligation owed to broadcasters | The resolution of private disputes before the public | The selection of a new presenter for the programme
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established understanding of a report attributed to "sources close to" a celebrated couple. | It is an unverified claim from an unnamed person | It is an official statement issued by the couple | It is a judgment delivered by a court of law | It is a certainty, being broadcast on television
exam: Whereas a presenter speculates, upon the basis of a single photograph, that a public figure is expecting a child, determine the appropriate characterisation of such conduct. | Unkind conjecture concerning a private matter | A confirmed announcement of a medical fact | A customary courtesy extended to all guests | An obligation of the broadcaster under law
exam: Ascertain the conclusion which may properly be drawn from the laughter of a studio audience. | It does not of itself establish that the jokes are amusing | It proves conclusively and beyond doubt that the jokes are amusing | It indicates that the audience has been paid to weep | It establishes that the programme is broadcast live
